  "id": "DrugBank_DB01185 - MONDO_18068",
  "verbalization": "Fluoxymesterone, a synthetic steroid, is linked to Trisomy 13 through a chain of indications that involve hypogonadism and testosterone therapy. The evidence suggests that Fluoxymesterone may be associated with hypogonadism in males, which is treated with testosterone propionate or enanthate. These testosterone therapies are in turn linked to Trisomy 13, indicating a potential causal or associative pathway. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The presence of multiple pathways pointing to a shared indication chain implies that Fluoxymesterone could be involved in the pathophysiology of Trisomy 13 through its effects on hormone regulation. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of testosterone therapy in the development or manifestation of Trisomy 13, though further research is needed to establish a definitive link.",
